## Proof

We use the trigonometric identity
$$\sin^3\theta = \frac{3\sin\theta - \sin 3\theta}{4}.$$

Applying this with $\theta = nx$,
$$\sin^3(nx) = \frac{3\sin(nx) - \sin(3nx)}{4}.$$

Therefore
$$\int_a^b f(x)\sin^3(nx)\,dx = \frac{3}{4}\int_a^b f(x)\sin(nx)\,dx - \frac{1}{4}\int_a^b f(x)\sin(3nx)\,dx.$$

Now we invoke the **Riemann–Lebesgue lemma**: if $g:[a,b]\to\mathbb{R}$ is integrable (in particular, continuous), then
$$\int_a^b g(x)\sin(\lambda x)\,dx \to 0 \quad\text{as } \lambda\to\infty.$$

Since $f$ is continuous on the compact interval $[a,b]$, it is integrable there. Applying the lemma:

- To the first integral with $\lambda = n$: as $n\to\infty$, $\displaystyle\int_a^b f(x)\sin(nx)\,dx \to 0$.
- To the second integral with $\lambda = 3n$: as $n\to\infty$, $3n\to\infty$, so $\displaystyle\int_a^b f(x)\sin(3nx)\,dx \to 0$.

Hence both terms on the right-hand side tend to $0$, and so
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\int_a^b f(x)\sin^3(nx)\,dx = \frac{3}{4}\cdot 0 - \frac{1}{4}\cdot 0 = 0.$$

The limit **exists** and equals $0$.

